# JIRA-to-RAML Generation Workflow

When the user provides one or more JIRA story IDs (e.g., `raml NGCRMI-1875` or `raml NGCRMI-1875 NGCRMI-1886`), execute this full workflow automatically without asking for extra input unless a value is genuinely ambiguous after reading both sources.

---

## Step 1 — Read Standards First (parallel)

Before touching any RAML files, read these two rules simultaneously:
- `gen-raml-implementation.mdc` — RAML structure, naming conventions, CommonLib usage, mandatory patterns
- `review-api-design-standards.mdc` — validation checklist to apply before finishing

---

## Step 2 — Fetch Each JIRA Story

For each story ID, call the JIRA REST API:

```
GET https://insulet.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/{STORY-ID}
Authorization: Basic base64(email:token)
Accept: application/json
```

Credentials are in `/Users/ankush.juneja/Downloads/insulet-atlassian-mcp-guardrails-main/.env`:
- `JIRA_EMAIL` — the account email
- `JIRA_TOKEN` — the API token

Fields to extract from the response:
| Field | JSON key |
|---|---|
| Summary | `fields.summary` |
| User story | `fields.description` |
| Acceptance Criteria | `fields.customfield_10308` |
| Technical Notes (has Confluence URL) | `fields.customfield_10364` |

Parse the Atlassian Document Format (ADF) nodes to plain text. Confluence URLs appear as `inlineCard` nodes or plain `text` nodes inside `customfield_10364`.

---

## Step 3 — Fetch the Confluence Design Page

Extract the Confluence page URL from the Technical Notes. The URL format is:
```
https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
```

Call the Confluence REST API using the same credentials:
```
GET https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/rest/api/content/{PAGE-ID}?expand=body.storage
Authorization: Basic base64(email:token)
Accept: application/json
```

Strip HTML from `body.storage.value` and parse for:
- Endpoint paths (e.g., `GET /consents`, `PUT /consents/{id}`)
- Input fields — query parameters, URI parameters, request body fields (name, type, mandatory flag)
- Output field mappings — API field name, data type, Salesforce source field, description
- HTTP status codes and their trigger conditions
- Salesforce object name (e.g., `ContactPointTypeConsent`)

If multiple JIRA stories reference the same Confluence page, fetch it only once.

---

## Step 4 — Resolve Design Decisions (automatic, no user prompt needed)

Apply these precedence rules in order:

| Decision | Rule |
|---|---|
| Field naming conflict between JIRA TC Guide and Confluence column header | **JIRA TC Guide wins** — e.g., if TC Guide says "Map Name to `consentName`", use `consentName` even if the Confluence table header says `name` |
| Response envelope | **Always `data`** as the top-level wrapper per `gen-raml-implementation.mdc` §5, regardless of what Confluence shows |
| HTTP verb | **Follow Confluence/JIRA stories exactly** — do not silently change PUT to PATCH or vice versa |
| PUT endpoints | **Inline body/responses** — `CommonLib.generic` only defines `get?`, `post?`, `patch?`; for PUT use explicit block with `is: [CommonLib.common-error-handler, CommonLib.common-headers, CommonLib.system-headers]` |
| Fields with no Salesforce mapping | **Remove them** — do not include fields that cannot be mapped to a Salesforce source field in the Confluence table |
| `referenceId` or other extra fields not in Confluence output | **Remove them** |
| `data` wrapper for Salesforce update responses | Apply `data` object wrapper with `id` and `success` per §5 |

---

## Step 5 — Generate or Update RAML

**Output location:** `agents/raml/examples/{api-spec-name}/`

The `api-spec-name` is derived from the Confluence/JIRA application name following the `{name}-{layer}-api-spec` pattern (no regional prefix).

If the spec folder **already exists**, update existing files in place and delete any stale fields or DataTypes not supported by the current Confluence design.

If it **does not exist**, scaffold an empty `exchange_modules/` directory and download the latest `common-fragment-library` from Anypoint Exchange to populate it (same download flow as Phase 7 of the full publish workflow).

### Required output files

```
{api-spec-name}/
├── {api-spec-name}.raml              ← root RAML (title, baseUri, types, resources)
├── dataTypes/
│   └── {DataTypeName}.raml           ← one file per DataType (TitleCase)
├── examples/
│   └── {ExampleName}Example.raml     ← one file per example (TitleCase)
└── exchange_modules/                 ← downloaded from Anypoint Exchange when scaffolding new spec
```

### Checklist before finishing

- [ ] All business endpoints use `CommonLib.generic` resourceType (except PUT — use inline)
- [ ] `CommonLib.common-error-handler` applied to every endpoint via `is:`
- [ ] No manually defined 4xx/5xx responses in root RAML
- [ ] Every response DataType and example has a top-level `data` wrapper
- [ ] Example files are TitleCase (e.g., `ConsentsGetResponseExample.raml`)
- [ ] No regional prefix in spec name
- [ ] `baseUri` declared
- [ ] `/ping` and `/health-check` included using `CommonLib.liveliness-probe` / `CommonLib.readiness-probe`
- [ ] `client-id-scheme` declared and applied via `securedBy`

---

## Step 6 — Persist Context to input_develop

After RAML generation is complete, write the following files to `project/input_develop/` so the develop agent has full business context without re-fetching from JIRA or Confluence.

### `project/input_develop/jira-{STORY-ID}.md`

```markdown
# JIRA: {STORY-ID} — {Summary}

## User Story
{parsed text from fields.description}

## Acceptance Criteria
{parsed text from fields.customfield_10308}

## Technical Notes
{parsed text from fields.customfield_10364}

## Confluence Design URL
{extracted URL}
```

### `project/input_develop/confluence-design-{STORY-ID}.md`

```markdown
# Confluence Design: {Page Title}
Source: {URL}

## Endpoints
{table or list of endpoints with HTTP verb, path, description}

## Request / Response Fields
{field tables — name, type, mandatory, description, Salesforce source}

## Downstream Systems
{systems identified in the design with their operations}

## HTTP Status Codes
{table of status codes and their trigger conditions}
```

**Rules:**
- If multiple JIRA stories were fetched, create one `jira-{STORY-ID}.md` per story (e.g., `jira-NGCRMI-1979.md`, `jira-NGCRMI-1980.md`).
- Create one `confluence-design-{STORY-ID}.md` per story (e.g., `confluence-design-NGCRMI-1979.md`, `confluence-design-NGCRMI-1980.md`). If a single Confluence page covers multiple stories, the content is identical across files.
- Overwrite any existing file with the same name.
- Write complete extracted content — do not summarise or truncate.
